A||N||10||Seanh||Couple Tire questions...||||1||
Z||000000||Seanh||09-29-2001||04:38 PM||||I am finally getting some new tires for the 86, as my rear ones are all shiny and theres no noticable tread left on them, radial drag slicks.<P>I am wanting to get some Kumho ECSTA Supra 712's. I reall have 3 options, none of which I really like.<P>1) 205/50R15's all around<BR>2) 205/55R15's all around<BR>3) 205/50R15's on front, and 205/55R15's on rear.<P>So should I put all the same size on the car? Or go with a little bit taller ones in the back? I want wider in the back, not really much taller. So which option should I go with?<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 4spd Auto, getting ready to install T-6 turbo.||||reg||1
Z||000001||KissMySSFiero||09-29-2001||05:20 PM||||if it were mycar i would put 225/50 all the way around if you want the same size.  if you want wider in the back i say go with 245/50<P>------------------<BR>JOEJOE Aol:SSFiero <BR>88FORMULA<BR>       <A HREF="mailto:SSFiero@Aol.com">SSFiero@Aol.com</A>        <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FORUMSIG1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||samoht||09-29-2001||06:28 PM||||i would go with the 205/55/15 all the way around ,just so it doesnt through your speedo off to much and anything wider will start to get harder to turn.. if you want to do it right and go with a low pro tire a 205/55/16 will keep your speedo perfect<P>85 GT 3.2<BR>205/55/16's||||reg||1
Z||000003||sqoach||09-29-2001||07:02 PM||||If you want just wider on the back and not any taller, go with a 205 55 front, and 225 50 on the rear. The rear is only slightly smaller, but you won't even be able to tell cuz it's so close. If it were me, I'd go a little taller and a little wider on the rear. And I wouldn't go any wider than the 225. Here's a link to show tire sizes if you don't know about it. It's a nice tool to have.  <A HREF="http://www.secret-secret.com/turbo/DIY/tirecalc/turbotire.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.secret-secret.com/turbo/DIY/tirecalc/turbotire.html</A> <P>------------------<BR>-Adam<BR><FONT COLOR="BLUE">sqoach@yahoo.com</FONT><BR>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sqoach.gif">         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">            <BR>^^^^^^<B>new project</B>^^^^^^<BR><LI><FONT SIZE="1">84 SE (FOR SALE)*SOLD*<BR><LI>85 Coupe (parts car)($50)*sale pending*<BR><LI>86 SC</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Rick 88||09-29-2001||08:28 PM||||According to a tire calculator, you should use a 225/45/15 rear to keep the overall diamater the same as the 205/50/15 front you selected while adding additional width.<P>Rick 88||||reg||1
Z||000005||Seanh||09-30-2001||01:42 PM||||I would love to have some 225/s or 235's on the back, but they don't make them in the type of tire I want, or I would get them. My only options are 205's. Mainly I'm looking for some tires that will make the car handle excellent in dry conditions. So I don't want too wide of tires or they will make it hard to turn I'm sure. So what should I do, look for some other type of tires, or put 205/55's all around? I am definatly going to buy from Tirerack.com, as I have had many excellent experiences with them in the past and love their service and prices. What tires would you reccomend? Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000006||Rick 88||09-30-2001||05:20 PM||||Do a search for the Falken tire site. The Ziex ZE-502's and GRB's are offered in many sizes. These tires are also reasonably priced and have good treadwear ratings. The Sumitomo HTRZ II's are aggressive tires priced competitively but the only have a 200 treadwear rating. My Ziex's have a 300 rating and are holding up very well.<P>Hope this helps,<BR>Rick 88||||reg||1
Z||000007||2birds||09-30-2001||11:04 PM||||Why do you want such a short tire on the back?  Unless you've lowered it a lot, it will have so much air gap it look like a Ford product (I know, I own two).  <BR>I lowered mine 1.5 inches and run 225/60's out back, 215/60's up front.  Steering is great, and the tires tuck up nicely.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Seanh||09-30-2001||11:56 PM||||I don't have to have 55's or 50's, Looking at the available options for 60's I like them. I'm looking at the Yokohama AVS dB's, 205's on the front and 225's on the back. Does that sound ok? I like wide tires on the back and easy to turn ones on the front.||||reg||1
Z||000009||2birds||10-01-2001||12:34 AM||||215/60/s are easy enough up front.  The other advantage is the # of choices and cheap price.  I ran BFG Comp T/A HR4 for a few years, at around $70 a tire, 215's and 225's.  Good tires, got about 40,000 out of them.  decent in the rain, too.  Mow I have some Viper GTH's from Sears on it.  About the same price, way better in the rain (directional).  They fit well on the stock 15x 7 rims, and don't stick out or rub (the fronts do just a little at full lock if you are lowered, and then only when brand new).  They also fill the wheel wells nicely, and stick like glue.  The 225/60's are only 1/2" taller than the stock 215's, so my speedo isn't off by much at all. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||ray b||10-01-2001||02:39 AM||||225-50-15 the speedo was spot on ck by radar sizn at 30 and 45.in a 87 se auto.<BR>only 45-15 i found are 195 koko's they would work on the front and i can't see a gap when i am in the car.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||3
